# Who reviews what.
#
# Previously this file read `* @everyone`, which meant nobody owned anything in
# particular and any one of us could merge — or revert — another's work without
# them seeing it. That is not a trust problem to solve with goodwill; it is a
# missing guardrail, and it cost the team a day.
#
# Last match wins, so the catch-all is first and the lanes follow.
#
# Every lane names TWO people, and that is deliberate. GitHub never lets you
# approve your own pull request, so a lane with a single owner cannot be
# merged by the person who owns it — they would be locked out of their own
# work. The second name is the reviewer of last resort, not a co-author.
#
# The protection still holds where it matters: whoever opens the pull request
# cannot approve it, so a change to someone else's lane always needs a pair of
# eyes from outside it.

# Anything not claimed below still needs a second pair of eyes.
